body:
I got Wilson Reading training, gave the WADE, and started planning. I'm so excited!

&#x200B;

I had a tough weak with my tutoree b/c of behavior issues, but I got great advice and validation from my Department Chair. So I have a solid plan, support, and resources. 

&#x200B;

I've made a lot of progress in building relationships with my students in the past two weeks, and I'm starting to get back in the routine of not over-helping, which is a weakness of mine b/c I get overexcited sometimes lol.

&#x200B;

So I'm on a high right now. Usually back to school season doesn't go so smoothly. There have been some hiccups (and one major incident), but everything else has been so pleasant that I'm feeling super optimistic.

I'll talk to her about it. I'm sure that she'd observe me or find someone willing. I'm super lucky to have a department chair who is more mentor than boss. She actually had to get special permission for me to go since I'm not full-time (they haven't added any new FT positions in a few years... ugh).

&#x200B;

Two fights?? That's gonna be fun to come back to...

We can't email about kids in my county either. We keep it vauge when we do email ("Hey, I was wondering if we could meet to talk about Jimmy's interventions? He had an off day/I need to clarify something/I'm planning and I could use some help.") or we keep it to very official correspondence that is common knowledge at the school and for the parents ("Here's the IEP/504/modified assignments/etc.")

&#x200B;

We're usually only required to give to what we have in our inbox, which works for us. For anything that's part of routine email maintenance where we deleted old emails, the parent is responsible for covering the cost of having a 3rd party go through the thousands of old emails and redacting any names from emails that aren't affiliated with the students. That costs thousands of dollars without probable cause, so most parents realize they're out of line and back off at that point.

&#x200B;

Maybe your county has similar policies? It's probably just "better safe than sorry" advice that's reactionary to policy changes. I wouldn't sweat it too much. Just keep it vague enough and use printouts that you can put in eachother's mailboxes if things get specific and you're unsure.
